Native Zero/Ezero compiler command

compiler-cli.zero is the complete original Zero implementation. Compile it for
Linux x86-64 or Windows x64 and place identical native copies at zero/ezero
(zero.exe/ezero.exe on Windows). Neither executable invokes a shell, command
interpreter, subprocess, external compiler or external language runtime.

Commands:
  zero INPUT.zero OUTPUT [TARGET]
  ezero INPUT.ezero OUTPUT [TARGET]
  zero --help

The default target matches the host: --host on Linux, --windows on Windows.
Explicit targets are --host, --windows, --raw, --raw32, --efi, --efi32, --real,
--app64 and --app32. An executable renamed to another basename can use an
explicit first argument zero or ezero to select the language. Source arguments
named zero/ezero remain normal filenames for the standard named commands.

Required files relative to the real executable's parent directory:
  lib/zero64.app
  lib/frontend64.app
  lib/pack64.app
  lib/runtime-prefix.zero.part
  lib/runtime.zero.part
These are compiled/source components of this same original Zero/Ezero toolchain.
The executable and lib directory can be relocated together. The current working
directory is irrelevant to companion lookup. Source/output paths remain relative
to the caller's current directory and support spaces and UTF-8 host paths.

The command reads source into bounded buffers, maps a compiler image as RW,
validates every ZEROAPP1 header field and its exact length, switches the mapping
to RX, then calls it directly with an owned context and stack. Ezero uses the
frontend, packer, runtime prefix/initializer/body and Zero compiler in sequence.
The command never requests RWX memory. It releases host mappings after returning.

Zero input is limited to 262144 bytes; Ezero input to 65536. A compiler image
must fit 1 MiB. The owned workspace is 8 MiB, including a 6 MiB application stack.
Generated Zero source is limited to 262144 bytes and target output to 491520.
The frontend/package and initializer buffers remain independently bounded.

Compilation must finish before the output file is created. Output creation is
exclusive; existing files, directories and final symlinks are refused. Linux
output permissions are 0700 reduced by the user's umask. Write/close failures
attempt to remove only the newly created output. Completed compilation does not
imply power-loss durability, process isolation or validity of arbitrary edited
compiler application bodies. ZEROAPP1 applications share this process's memory.

Ezero frontend diagnostics retain their original source-line message. The initial
Zero compiler has only a failure status, so its command reports a compile failure
without inventing a source location. Messages use the current console/stdout.
This command builds executables; it does not launch the resulting program.

Current original Zero verification:
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
See checks/README.ztext for source/ABI checks and native filesystem coverage.
This replaces removed foreign-language test runners. Host checks do not prove
firmware boot, physical hardware support or arbitrary edited app isolation.
